course: chinese-street
lesson: 16
title: Happy, angry, bored, scared
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your friend Lín bursts out of school shouting "jīntiān hǎo kāixīn!" — "today was so much fun!" — and you hear "KIGH-shin", which means happy, literally "open heart".
explain: Feelings in Mandarin are short and loud: "shēngqì" (SHUNG-CHEE) is cross, "wúliáo" (WOO-lyow, both rising) is bored, "wǒ hǎo pà" (waw HOW PAH) is "I'm really scared", "nánguò" is sad.
explain: Kids love drama: "qì sǐ wǒ le!" is literally "angry me to death", but nobody is dying — sǐ (death) just means "really, really", like "lèi sǐ le", dead tired.
explain: Some feelings bite if you aim them at a person: "hǎo wúliáo" is "I'm so bored", but "nǐ zhēn wúliáo" is "you're so boring"; and "tǎoyàn!" said laughing is "stop it, you!", said coldly it is real dislike.
explain: When Lín looks sad, the kind thing is "zěnme le?" — what's up? — and when he's cross, "bié shēngqì", don't be cross.
fact: "kāixīn" (KIGH-shin) means happy, having fun, literally "open heart", and is the everyday word kids use.
fact: "gāoxìng" means glad or pleased and is a little more grown-up; "rènshi nǐ hěn gāoxìng" means "nice to meet you", and "tā bù gāoxìng le" means "he's sulking".
fact: "shēngqì" means angry or cross; "nǐ shēngqì le ma?" asks "are you cross?", and "bié shēngqì" (don't be cross) is the first step to making up.
fact: "qì sǐ wǒ le" is a dramatic "it's driving me mad!"; sǐ (death) is used for "really, really", as in "lèi sǐ le", dead tired.
fact: "wúliáo" (both syllables rising) means bored or boring: "hǎo wúliáo a" is "I'm so bored", and the cheerful answer is "wǒmen wánr ba!", let's play.
fact: "nǐ zhēn wúliáo" said about a person is a put-down: "you're so boring" or "you're so silly".
fact: "wǒ hǎo pà" means "I'm really scared", with pà falling sharply; the kind answer is "bié pà", don't be scared, and it is fine for boys to say it too.
fact: "nánguò" means sad, literally "hard to get through"; it is the right word for a dead pet or a sick granny, and the kind answer is "zěnme le?".
fact: "xiǎng kū" means "I feel like crying"; kū (cry) is high and level, while kǔ (dipping) means bitter, as in "chī kǔ", to put up with hardship.
fact: "xǐhuan" means to like: "wǒ xǐhuan nǐ de gǒu" is "I like your dog"; but "wǒ xǐhuan nǐ" between schoolkids sounds like a crush, so to a friend say "nǐ shì wǒ de hǎo péngyou".
fact: "bù xǐhuan" means "don't like"; saying it about food at the friend's table is rude, so say "wǒ bú tài è", I'm not very hungry.
fact: "tǎoyàn!" said laughing when teased means "stop it, you!"; said coldly about a person it really means "I can't stand you".
fact: "hǎo xiànmù" means "I'm so jealous" in a nice way, when a friend gets a new toy; "jídù" is the nasty kind of jealousy.
exercise: Lín runs out of school shouting "jīntiān hǎo kāixīn!". How does he feel? | Happy, it was great fun | Cross, it was a bad day | Bored, nothing happened | Scared of something
exercise: What does "kāixīn" mean word for word? | Open heart | Big smile | High voice | Warm hands
exercise: Lín's dad says "rènshi nǐ hěn gāoxìng" when he meets you. What is he saying? | Nice to meet you | Please come in | You are very tall | Have you eaten yet
exercise: Lín's sister says "tā bù gāoxìng le" about Lín. What is Lín doing? | Sulking, in a mood | Laughing his head off | Sleeping on the sofa | Eating all the sweets
exercise: Lín stamps his foot and you ask "nǐ shēngqì le ma?". What are you asking? | Are you cross? | Are you hungry? | Are you tired? | Are you scared?
exercise: Lín's game crashed again: "qì sǐ wǒ le!". What does he mean? | It's driving me mad! | I'm going to die! | I'm dead tired! | I'm so scared!
exercise: It's raining and Lín sighs "hǎo wúliáo a". What's a good answer? | wǒmen wánr ba! | bié pà! | bié shēngqì! | xiǎng kū!
exercise: You hear "WOO-lyow", both bits going up. Which word was it? | wúliáo, bored | wǒ lèi, I'm tired | wǔ, five | wánr, play
exercise: On the high slide Lín's little sister whispers "wǒ hǎo pà". What do you say? | bié pà | bié shēngqì | hǎo wúliáo | qì sǐ wǒ le
exercise: Lín's hamster died and he says "wǒ hěn nánguò". How does he feel? | Sad | Bored | Cross | Excited
exercise: Your friend is quiet and looks sad. Which question is kindest? | zěnme le? | nǐ zhēn wúliáo! | nǐ jǐ suì le? | chī le ma?
exercise: After falling off his bike, Lín says "xiǎng kū". What does he mean? | I feel like crying | I feel like eating | I want to go home | I want to try again
exercise: kū is high and level. What do you say if your voice dips, kǔ? | Bitter | Cry | Cool | Dog
exercise: You want to say "I like your dog". Which is right? | wǒ xǐhuan nǐ de gǒu | wǒ bù xǐhuan nǐ de gǒu | wǒ hǎo pà nǐ de gǒu | wǒ xiǎng nǐ de gǒu
exercise: Lín's mum serves something you don't like. What is the polite thing to say? | wǒ bú tài è | wǒ bù xǐhuan | tǎoyàn! | hǎo wúliáo
exercise: Lín tickles his sister and she squeals "tǎoyàn!" laughing. What does she mean? | Stop it, you! | I hate you forever | I'm so bored | Help, I'm scared
exercise: Lín got a new bike and you say "hǎo xiànmù!". What are you saying? | I'm so jealous (in a nice way) | I hate you for getting that bike | That bike is ugly, I don't want one | Can I have it, please
exercise: Which word is the nasty kind of jealousy? | jídù | xiànmù | kāixīn | gāoxìng
test: my mate says "qì sǐ wǒ le" means someone actually died. right? | No, it's a dramatic "it's driving me mad" | Yes, it means someone in the house has died | Yes, it means "I'm dead tired" | No, it means "I'm so happy"
test: Lín yells "tǎoyàn!" with a straight cold face, not laughing, at a boy who keeps pushing him. What does it mean this time? | Real dislike: "I can't stand you" | A friendly "stop it, you!" | "Thank you very much" | "I'm bored, let's play"
test: You meant "I'm bored" but said "nǐ zhēn wúliáo" to Lín. What did he hear? | You're so boring | I'm so bored | Let's play something | You're so funny
test: In a cartoon a boy shouts "wǒ xǐhuan nǐ!" to his best mate in front of the class and everyone giggles. Why? | It sounds like a crush; mates say "good friend" | It is a swear word in Chinese, a really bad one | It means "I don't like you" | It means "I'm hungry, give me your lunch"
test: Lín is cross after losing at cards. What's the first step to making up? | Say "bié shēngqì" and smile | Say "nǐ zhēn wúliáo" | Say "qì sǐ wǒ le" | Say "hǎo xiànmù"
test: ugh scary film, my cousin's hiding behind the sofa and goes "wǒ hǎo pà". boys don't get scared, innit? | Wrong, anyone gets scared; say "bié pà" | Right, only girls ever say "wǒ hǎo pà" | Right, it means he's bored of the film | Wrong, it means he's angry
test: Which pair is "happy, having fun" and "glad, a bit more grown-up"? | kāixīn and gāoxìng | nánguò and shēngqì | wúliáo and tǎoyàn | xiànmù and jídù
test: Lín's granny is in hospital. Which word fits how he feels? | nánguò | kāixīn | xīngfèn | wúliáo
test: Your friend says "wǒ hǎo xiànmù nǐ" when you show your new trainers. Is he being nasty? | No, it's a friendly "I'm so jealous" | Yes, it's the nasty jealousy word, he's raging | Yes, he says he hates you | No, he says he's bored
test: What would happen if you said "bù xǐhuan" loudly about the soup at Lín's table? | It would sound rude to his mum | His mum would give you more soup | Everyone would think you're full | Nothing, it means "thank you"
test: Lín's sister mixes up kū and kǔ: "wǒ xiǎng kǔ". What did she nearly say? | Something about bitter, not crying | Something about a dog | Something about cool | Something about a cow
exam: Identify the expression whereby a Mandarin-speaking child customarily signifies enjoyment, its literal sense being "open heart". | kāixīn | nánguò | wúliáo | shēngqì
exam: It is given that the phrase "qì sǐ wǒ le" contains the element sǐ. Select the statement that accurately characterises the function of that element. | It intensifies the feeling and does not denote actual death | It denotes the death of the speaker, literally understood | It denotes the death of a third party | It renders the utterance a question
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the utterance "nǐ zhēn wúliáo" when directed at a person? | It is a disparaging remark meaning "you are so boring" | It is a declaration that the speaker himself is bored | It is an invitation to play | It is a formal greeting
exam: Ascertain the appropriate response to a companion who declares "wǒ hǎo pà". | bié pà | bié shēngqì | hǎo xiànmù | tǎoyàn
